{"type":"text","text":"Done. World 1-1's world and level numbers live in the level-state block at `$0750`, right beside the already-known lives counter `$075A` (which read **1** after one intentional death, confirming the neighborhood).\n\n## Results\n\n| Variable | Address | Encoding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **World number** (1st digit of `WORLD`) | `$075F` | 1 byte, **0-based** — 0 = World 1; HUD/card prints value+1 |\n| **Level number** (2nd digit of `WORLD`) | `$075C` | 1 byte, **0-based** — 0 = 1st level (e.g. 1-1); HUD prints value+1 |\n\n**Baseline:** in `WORLD 1-1` the block read `$075A`=1 (lives), `$075E`=0 (coin counter), `$075C`=0, `$075F`=0 — both bytes zero, i.e. 0-based.\n\n**How I found them (differential search, no reference):** the values never move by playing, so I snapshot before/after a death (filter `unchanged`) and narrowed the unchanged survivors by the free `0` value, then proved the candidates by write and a redraw event (a lost life reloads the level and the `WORLD` card/HUD is redrawn from these bytes).\n\n**Proof screenshots (write → display):**\n1. Wrote `$075F`=4 **and** `$075C`=2 → HUD showed **WORLD 5-3**, and the game actually loaded a castle (5-3).\n2. Isolated the level byte: restored to 1-1, wrote `$075C`=4 alone (`$075F` left 0) → HUD showed **WORLD 1-5**; only the second digit moved.\n3. Isolated the world byte: wrote `$075F`=6 alone (`$075C` left 4) → HUD showed **WORLD 7-5**; only the first digit moved.\n\nSo each of the two digits is controlled by exactly one address, and the display is value+1 (0-based storage).\n\nBoth were recorded with `record_mapping` (mapping_type `address`, source `differential search`), and `promote_mappings` was called once — the shareable mapping file now has 12 addresses. I restored the 1-1 save state and left the game running; note the writes were RAM-only, so they vanish on reload (the ROM ledger is untouched)."}
